{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>גם אותכה הרגתי (also you I would have killed).</b> Rashi explained: this is an inverted verse, and it is as though it says גם הרגתי אותך. And an explanation is needed for his interpretation — this is what it says: for if we interpret as he wrote, it would imply 'also you I killed along with the she-donkey,' and if so, how could it afterward say ואותה החייתי? But now that we invert it, we can interpret thus: 'I called you to account not only for hindering me, but also for the killing' — that is, not only would I have done to you that I would have held you back here, but I would also have killed you; ואותה החייתי — for once he would be dead, there is no concern for his honor so much. But now, because she spoke and rebuked you and you could not withstand her rebuke — I killed her, etc. — for the Place has mercy on the dignity of creatures.'" "<b>ואותה החייתי (and her I kept alive).</b> It appears that he also killed the youths, for they are not mentioned at all here."], :book :numbers, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The phrase הרגתיה כו' ('I killed her, etc.') appears to refer to killing the donkey; the square brackets are in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:10:00.094067342Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}